        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Workouts & Training", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/training/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Treadmill Walking Workouts", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/treadmill-walking-workouts/" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a good treadmill walking workout?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "A good starting workout is 5 minutes easy and flat, 20 to 30 minutes at a brisk pace (typically 3 to 3.5 mph) at 2 to 5% incline, then 5 minutes easy. Once that is comfortable, add variety across the week — incline intervals, a pyramid and a longer easy walk — and progress duration before grade and grade before speed.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How many times a week should I do treadmill walking workouts?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Three to five sessions a week suits most people. Keep most of them steady or easy, make one or two harder (incline intervals or a pyramid), and include one longer easy walk. Avoid hard steep sessions on consecutive days, because calves and Achilles tendons recover more slowly than your heart and lungs.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is a good incline treadmill walking workout?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Incline intervals are a good place to start: after a 5-minute flat warm-up, alternate 2 minutes at 6 to 10% with 2 minutes at 1 to 3% for five rounds at a constant brisk speed, then cool down. Build to a steady hill climb or the 12-3-30 workout only after several weeks of interval and pyramid sessions.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is walking on a treadmill a good workout?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, if it has some structure. Brisk walking counts as moderate-intensity exercise, and adding incline makes it considerably harder without the impact of running. A plan that mixes steady walks, harder incline sessions and one long easy walk — and progresses gradually — is a genuinely effective workout for most people.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How long should a treadmill walking workout be?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Most sessions work well at 30 to 45 minutes including a warm-up and cool-down, with one longer easy walk of 45 to 75 minutes a week. Total weekly minutes matter more than any single session; if you are starting out, 20 to 25 minutes is a perfectly good session to build from.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can I do walking workouts on a walking pad with no incline?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Partly. Steady brisk walks, long easy walks and speed-walk intervals all work at 0%, provided the pad reaches your fast walking pace. Incline intervals, pyramids and hill climbs need a powered incline, so on a flat pad replace those with extra speed-walk sessions or longer walks.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      description: description,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-10-06",
      dateModified: "2026-10-06",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
